+# Lansing Codes Beginner Resources Contributing Guide
+
+Thanks for helping people find a good starting point for learning to code.
+Please read the [Lansing Codes Code of Conduct](https://www.lansing.codes/code-of-conduct/)
+before joining a discussion or submitting a change.
+
+## Contents
+
+- [Code of Conduct](#code-of-conduct)
+- [Issue Reporting Guidelines](#issue-reporting-guidelines)
+- [Pull Request Guidelines](#pull-request-guidelines)
+- [Development Setup](#development-setup)
+- [Testing Locally](#testing-locally)
+- [Deployments](#deployments)
+
+## Code of Conduct
+
+The [Code of Conduct](https://www.lansing.codes/code-of-conduct/) applies to
+issues, pull requests, and community conversations.
+
+## Issue Reporting Guidelines
+
+Search [existing issues](https://github.com/lansingcodes/beginner-resources/issues)
+before filing a new one. Use the [bug report
+template](https://github.com/lansingcodes/beginner-resources/issues/new?template=bug_report.md)
+for a broken link or incorrect description. Include the affected README
+section, the URL, what you observed, and what you expected. Use the [feature
+request template](https://github.com/lansingcodes/beginner-resources/issues/new?template=feature_request.md)
+to propose a new category or other larger change.
+
+For a new learning resource, explain who it helps, what it teaches, whether it
+is free or paid, and why it belongs in the proposed category. Link directly to
+the resource. Avoid referral links and unverified claims about price or access.
+
+## Pull Request Guidelines
+
+1. Work in a branch on your fork and send a pull request to `main`.
+2. Keep unrelated resource additions and fixes in separate pull requests.
+3. Link the relevant issue and explain the value of each new resource or the
+   reason for removing or changing an existing one.
+4. Check spelling, Markdown formatting, and links before submitting. Include
+   the checks you ran and their results in the pull request.
+5. Request a maintainer review. A maintainer decides when the change is ready
+   to merge.
+
+If a resource requires an account, payment, or a particular device, say so in
+its description. Do not add personal referral codes, credentials, or tracking
+links. Follow the tone and category structure already used in the README.
+
+## Development Setup
+
+You need [Git](https://git-scm.com/downloads), a text editor with Markdown
+preview (such as [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/)), and a browser.
+[Node.js and npm](https://nodejs.org/) are optional for the `live-server`
+preview below. There is no application build or dependency install in this
+repository; its published content is the README on GitHub.
+
+Fork [beginner-resources](https://github.com/lansingcodes/beginner-resources),
+then clone your fork and create a branch:
+
+```sh
+git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/beginner-resources.git
+cd beginner-resources
+git switch -c docs/describe-your-change
+```
+
+Edit `README.md` and, if needed, supporting files in `.github/`. Keep the
+original source URL visible in the Markdown so others can verify it.
+
+## Testing Locally
+
+Use your editor's Markdown preview to check that headings, lists, and links
+render correctly. To serve the public README locally, run this from the
+repository root:
+
+```sh
+npx --yes live-server --no-browser --port=8080
+```
+
+Open `http://127.0.0.1:8080/README.md` to confirm the file is served, then
+open each changed external link in a browser. `live-server` serves the Markdown
+source, not GitHub's rendered README, and does not serve files under `.github/`.
+Preview those files in your editor and check the rendered Markdown in the pull
+request's GitHub preview. Stop the server with Ctrl+C.
+
+No automated test suite is configured for this documentation repository. For
+link fixes, record the destination and the observed response or page in the
+pull request. If a site blocks automated requests, describe a browser check
+instead of claiming a link test passed.
+
+## Deployments
+
+There is no separate website deployment for this repository. After a pull
+request is reviewed and merged into `main`, GitHub displays the updated README
+on the repository's front page. Contributors should use pull requests rather
+than pushing directly to `main`; verify the final rendered page after merge.
